## Common mistakes

### Illogical focus order on menu

On a college's application platform, the user activates a button to open the menu on the left hand side. However, when the user continues to tab, focus moves through the page behind the menu, rather than the menu.

This particular example fails because after opening the menu, the user needs to tab backwards to reach the items in it. When a user opens a menu, they would expect their next tab to be within the menu.

<%= image_tag "../images/243/243-fail-illogical-menu-ordering.png", { :alt => 'A menu on a college website, partially covering up a link which has keyboard focus, and some course details.' } %>

### Other common mistakes

The following scenarios may also have an impact on focus order:

* When styles are disabled, the page's focus order may no longer make sense.
* It can be distracting if non-interactive elements (such as headings) receive keyboard focus.
* When zoomed in or viewing on a mobile device, the tabbing order may not match the visual order.

## Related success criteria

* [1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence (A)](1.3.2.html) relates to the reading order of all meaningful content on the page, not just focusable elements
* [2.1.1 Keyboard (A)](2.1.1.html) requires everything to work with a keyboard, regardless of whether the focus is in a logical order or not.
* [2.4.7 Focus Visible (AA)](2.4.7.html) requires that keyboard focus is always visible, regardless of the order.
* [2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) (AA)](2.4.11.html) requires that elements are not covered by something else on screen when they do receive focus.
* [3.2.1 On Focus (A)](3.2.1.html) requires focus to stay where it is when an element receives focus.
* [3.2.2 On Input (A)](3.2.2.html) requires focus to stay where it is when something interactive is changed, like a toggle switch.

